{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>אַשְׁרֵךְ</b>. This word occurs without a construct bound noun in the plural, like <b>אַשְׁרֵי הָאִישׁ</b>, and once in construct in the plural, like <b>אַשְׁרָיו</b> ('happy is he'), and elsewhere in the singular, <b>אַשְׁרֵהוּ</b>. Rabbi Moses the Priest said, may his memory be for a blessing, that <b>אַשְׁרֵהוּ</b> is plural, and likewise <b>עֵינָיו</b>, <b>יָדָהוּ</b>, <b>גְּבוּרָתוֹ</b> — when he prays on behalf of his fellow. And his proofs: 'the strength of the eyes of YHWH' — the ways of a man — and he forgot, 'behold, the eye of YHWH is upon them that fear Him.' Likewise <b>יָדָהוּ</b> is singular, and <b>גְּבוּרָתוֹ</b> likewise, a shield for every mighty one who has it. And on account of the phrase 'when he prays on behalf of his fellow' the world is turned upside down: if so, 'in the hand of his fellow' — were there two? And what is the difference between <b>רֵעוֹ</b> and <b>רֵעֵהוּ</b>? — that <b>הוּא</b> may also occur with plural verbs: 'they pursue, they pursue him.' Perhaps Job prayed on behalf of each one of his fellows individually — a prayer in and of itself — and each one of them took seven bulls and seven rams, and this proves that <b>אַשְׁרֵהוּ</b> is singular. <b>אַשְׁרֵךְ אֶרֶץ</b>." "<b>And the meaning of בֶּן חוֹרִים</b>. That he does great deeds, the opposite of a <b>בֶּן בְּלִיָּעַל</b>. One of the commentators said: it is known that there is nothing in the eyes like the color white, which resembles light, and there is nothing lower than black. And Scripture likens great men to white and lesser men, who have no virtue or greatness, to darkness. And he interprets <b>חוֹרִים</b> as white ones, like <b>חוֹר כַּרְפַּס</b>, and likewise he interprets <b>אֹרְגִים חוֹרֵי</b> as white. <b>סָלֵי חוֹרִים</b> — white, and <b>לְבָן</b> is the Aramaic equivalent, <b>חִיוַר</b>. And the opposite of <b>חוֹרִים</b> is <b>חֲשׁוּכִים</b>: 'he shall not stand before darkness.'" "Some interpret <b>וְלֹא בַשִּׂיתִי</b> as <b>בַּשִּׂיא</b> (lordship). And one says that <b>שִּׂיתִי</b> is like <b>דְּלוּת</b> (poverty), like 'their tongue cleaves in thirst' (<b>וְנִשְׁתּוּ</b>). And the meaning is that all their deeds are in strength, and the meaning of attaching <b>גְּבוּרָה</b> to eating is that they eat until the first food is desired and digested. And the meaning is that they do not eat except for necessity, not for the sake of pleasure."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The comment on Job's prayer alludes to Job 42:8–9; Ibn Ezra argues from the singular form of the pronominal suffix against Rabbi Moses the Priest's position. The sentence beginning 'and he forgot' refers to a proof-text the opponent overlooked.", 1 "The final phrase alludes to Ecclesiastes 10:16 (or possibly 10:4); the verse reference is implicit in the Hebrew and unresolved.", 2 "The reading of Ecclesiastes 10:17 has <b>וְלֹא בַשִּׂיתִי</b>; Ibn Ezra presents two interpretations of this difficult form."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:29:22.867759836Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}